{"storyId":"AMSD-1919","agent":"speckit","storyKind":"defect","title":"Go-Checkout Page - Email & Confirm Email Case Sensitivity","description":"On the Go-Checkout page, the Email and Confirm Email fields are compared case-sensitively (CheckoutForm.tsx:306, `value !== email`), so entering the same address with different letter casing (e.g. Name@Example.com vs name@example.com) incorrectly triggers a \"TRM774\" mismatch error. Comparison must be case-insensitive, and the same normalization must be applied to the value that is actually submitted downstream so a mixed-case email isn't silently altered or mismatched at submit time.","notes":"Openspec supplied no acceptanceCriteria (empty array) — I built the full list from the description, technicalNotes, and by reading src/components/pages/CheckoutPage/CheckoutForm.tsx directly (lines ~259-322) to confirm the defect: the `validate` rule on the `confirmEmail` field at line 306 does `value !== email` with no case normalization, and clearErrors on email onChange doesn't re-validate confirmEmail. I traced submission: CheckoutForm calls `handleSubmit(onSubmit)` (line 168) which passes raw, unmodified form values to the parent, confirming that normalization must also apply to the submitted values, not just the inline validator, per openspec's note. I did not find email-handling code in CheckoutPageComponentMapper.tsx or PurchaseConfirmationPageComponentMapper.tsx (grepped, no matches) — those files listed in technicalNotes appear unrelated to this specific validation path; only CheckoutForm.tsx is directly implicated.\n\nAdded AC covering: case-insensitive match acceptance, leading/trailing whitespace handling, mismatch still caught for genuinely different addresses, error clears/re-validates when either field changes, no visual alteration of user-typed casing in the input itself, and downstream submission normalization consistency. Flagged one AC (whitespace trimming) since it wasn't explicitly requested by openspec/description and is an assumption about scope. Flagged the \"submission normalization\" AC as needing product confirmation on exactly what normalized form (lowercase vs as-typed) should be sent downstream, since the story description says \"not accidentally split by casing\" but doesn't specify the canonical casing to persist.","acceptanceCriteria":["Given the Email field contains \"Name@Example.com\" and the Confirm Email field contains \"name@example.com\", when the user submits or blurs the Confirm Email field, then no mismatch error (TRM774) is shown.","Given the Email field contains \"name@example.com\" and the Confirm Email field contains \"NAME@EXAMPLE.COM\", when validation runs, then the two values are treated as matching and the form can proceed.","Given the Email field contains \"name@example.com\" and the Confirm Email field contains \"other@example.com\" (a genuinely different address, ignoring case), when validation runs, then the mismatch error (TRM774) is still shown.","Given a mismatch error is currently shown on Confirm Email, when the user edits the Email field to a value that now matches Confirm Email (ignoring case), then the mismatch error is cleared without requiring the user to re-type Confirm Email.","Given the user has typed an email address in mixed case into either the Email or Confirm Email field, when the field is displayed, then the characters are shown exactly as typed (case-insensitive comparison does not alter the displayed input value).","Given Email and Confirm Email contain the same address differing only by case, when the form is submitted, then the email value sent downstream (e.g. to the confirmation page / backend) is consistent between what was validated and what was submitted, and is not split or mismatched due to casing differences between the two fields.","Given the Confirm Email field is empty and the Email field has a value, when validation runs, then the existing required-field behavior is unchanged (no case-sensitivity-related regression to the required rule).","Given the Email field contains a value with leading or trailing whitespace and Confirm Email contains the same address without whitespace, when validation runs, then the two values are still treated as matching."],"acAddedBySpeckit":["Given the Email field contains \"Name@Example.com\" and the Confirm Email field contains \"name@example.com\", when the user submits or blurs the Confirm Email field, then no mismatch error (TRM774) is shown.","Given the Email field contains \"name@example.com\" and the Confirm Email field contains \"NAME@EXAMPLE.COM\", when validation runs, then the two values are treated as matching and the form can proceed.","Given the Email field contains \"name@example.com\" and the Confirm Email field contains \"other@example.com\" (a genuinely different address, ignoring case), when validation runs, then the mismatch error (TRM774) is still shown.","Given a mismatch error is currently shown on Confirm Email, when the user edits the Email field to a value that now matches Confirm Email (ignoring case), then the mismatch error is cleared without requiring the user to re-type Confirm Email.","Given the user has typed an email address in mixed case into either the Email or Confirm Email field, when the field is displayed, then the characters are shown exactly as typed (case-insensitive comparison does not alter the displayed input value).","Given Email and Confirm Email contain the same address differing only by case, when the form is submitted, then the email value sent downstream (e.g. to the confirmation page / backend) is consistent between what was validated and what was submitted, and is not split or mismatched due to casing differences between the two fields.","Given the Confirm Email field is empty and the Email field has a value, when validation runs, then the existing required-field behavior is unchanged (no case-sensitivity-related regression to the required rule).","Given the Email field contains a value with leading or trailing whitespace and Confirm Email contains the same address without whitespace, when validation runs, then the two values are still treated as matching."],"acModifiedBySpeckit":[],"acFlagged":[{"criterion":"Given the Email field contains a value with leading or trailing whitespace and Confirm Email contains the same address without whitespace, when validation runs, then the two values are still treated as matching.","flag":"Whitespace trimming was not explicitly requested by the story description or openspec's notes (which only mention case normalization); confirm with product whether trimming is in scope for this fix or should be a separate concern."},{"criterion":"Given Email and Confirm Email contain the same address differing only by case, when the form is submitted, then the email value sent downstream (e.g. to the confirmation page / backend) is consistent between what was validated and what was submitted, and is not split or mismatched due to casing differences between the two fields.","flag":"Openspec's notes say submission-time normalization should be applied 'consistently' but don't specify the canonical casing to persist (as-typed vs lowercased). Needs product/design confirmation on which casing is sent to downstream systems and displayed on the confirmation page."}],"verificationCriteriaDetail":[{"criterion":"Entering the same email address with different letter casing in Email and Confirm Email does not show a mismatch error and allows the user to proceed with checkout.","observer":"end user","surface":"Go-Checkout page form (Email and Confirm Email fields and any inline validation message)"},{"criterion":"Entering two genuinely different email addresses (differing by more than case) in Email and Confirm Email still shows the TRM774 mismatch error.","observer":"end user","surface":"Go-Checkout page form (Confirm Email field inline validation message)"},{"criterion":"After fixing the Email field so it matches Confirm Email (ignoring case), the previously shown mismatch error disappears without needing to retype Confirm Email.","observer":"end user","surface":"Go-Checkout page form (Confirm Email field inline validation message)"},{"criterion":"The email address as typed by the user (including original casing) is submitted/passed through consistently to the next step (e.g. purchase confirmation page) without being altered or causing a downstream mismatch.","observer":"end user","surface":"Purchase confirmation page or downstream API payload showing the submitted email value","setup":"User completes checkout with Email and Confirm Email differing only by case, then submits the form."}]}

